/* time o(n) space o(1)
intuition 
Count frequencies of s1 in an array c1[26].

Initialize a window in s2 of length s1.length() and count its frequencies in c2[26].

Compute “matches” = how many characters have equal frequency in both arrays (c1[i] == c2[i]).

If all 26 characters match, the first window is already a permutation → return true.

Slide the window one step at a time through s2:

Add the next character entering the window (right side).

Update matches for that character (it may start or stop matching).

Remove the character leaving the window (left side).

Update matches for that character as well.

After each slide, if matches == 26, the current window is a perfect frequency match → permutation found.

If you finish sliding and never reach 26 matches → return false. */



class Solution {
    public boolean checkInclusion(String s1, String s2) {
        if(s1.length()>s2.length())return false;
        int[] c1 = new int[26];
        int[] c2 = new int[26];
        for(int i=0;i<s1.length();i++){
            c1[s1.charAt(i)-'a']++;
            c2[s2.charAt(i)-'a']++;
        }
        int matches = 0;
        for(int i=0;i<26;i++){
            if(c1[i]==c2[i])matches++;
        }
        int l=0;
        for(int r=s1.length();r<s2.length();r++){
            if(matches==26)return true;
            int val = s2.charAt(r)-'a';
            c2[val]++;
            if(c1[val]==c2[val])matches++;
            else if(c1[val] + 1 == c2[val])matches--;

            val = s2.charAt(l)-'a';
            c2[val]--;
            if(c1[val]==c2[val])matches++;
            else if(c1[val]-1==c2[val])matches--;
            l++;
        }
        return (matches == 26);
    }
}
